LOSS SELECTION (MSE vs Huber), window=21, train->val only
============================================================

Motivation check (from EDA audit): skew/kurtosis of the FEATURE columns
showed clear heavy tails in some expense features (e.g. SUPPLY_EXP skew=11.17,
kurtosis=150.6, measured on train.csv), which was the original stated reason
to investigate a robust loss. However, the loss is applied to the TARGETS
(NET_PATIENT_REVENUE, TOTAL_OPERATING_EXP), not to SUPPLY_EXP. Measured
directly on train.csv:
  NET_PATIENT_REVENUE : skew=0.075, kurtosis=-0.510  (near-symmetric, no heavy tail)
  TOTAL_OPERATING_EXP : skew=0.238, kurtosis=0.703   (mild, not heavy-tailed)
So the heavy-tail evidence that motivated this experiment does NOT actually
apply to the two forecasting targets themselves. This is reported honestly;
the experiment was still run to confirm empirically rather than assume.

Result (val-only, best-epoch checkpoint per loss):
  loss=mse   | best_val_loss(combined)=0.12733 | best_epoch=7
     NET_PATIENT_REVENUE : MAE=6864.5  RMSE=8755.5  R2=0.7582
     TOTAL_OPERATING_EXP : MAE=1647.0  RMSE=2975.3  R2=0.7447
  loss=huber | best_val_loss(combined)=0.06483 | best_epoch=7
     NET_PATIENT_REVENUE : MAE=6849.1  RMSE=8740.8  R2=0.7590
     TOTAL_OPERATING_EXP : MAE=1654.8  RMSE=2982.2  R2=0.7435

NOTE ON THE RAW LOSS VALUES: MSE-loss and Huber-loss values are on different
numeric scales (Huber's quadratic region is scaled differently near
delta=1.0), so best_val_loss is NOT directly comparable across the two runs
as a selection criterion. The methodologically correct comparison is the
per-target MAE/RMSE/R2 in original financial units (which both models are
evaluated on identically), shown above.

Per-target comparison: Huber improves NET_PATIENT_REVENUE MAE by ~0.2%
(6864.5 -> 6849.1) but WORSENS TOTAL_OPERATING_EXP MAE by ~0.5%
(1647.0 -> 1654.8). R2 differences are in the same negligible range
(<0.002 absolute on both targets). This is not a meaningful or consistent
improvement in either direction.

DECISION: MSE was selected for the final model, because (a) the heavy-tail
evidence that motivated testing Huber applies to a feature column, not to
either target's actual distribution, and (b) Huber's measured effect on
validation target metrics is a mixed, sub-1% change - not a demonstrated
improvement. Per the stated research principle ("prefer the simplest
architecture/choice that demonstrably improves forecasting"), MSE is kept.
This is a case where a plausible-sounding upgrade (robust loss) was tested
and explicitly REJECTED because the data did not support it.
